we begin this half hour with a visit to  windsor castle just west of london it's  where britain's queen elizabeth spends  many of her private weekends  it's also home to a stunning display of  flowers and trees  starting this weekend and lasting for  the next few weeks the public  will be allowed to get a rare look at  the castle gardens  and what makes them so special  windsor castle one of queen elizabeth's  official residences  home to ceremonies full of pageantry and  tradition  including the 2018 wedding of prince  harry  and meghan markle it's also known for  its stunning gardens  including one that's been closed to the  public for decades  until today for the first time in 40  years the queen is allowing commoners  the chance to explore her beloved east  terrace gardens  right in her own backyard various  generations of the royal family have  actually looked into this garden  from the apartments up above and also  it's been a quiet spot we're on the far  side from the town of windsor and the  main road  it's not overlooked so it gives a nice  sense  of privacy and a bit of a retreat as  well a nice quiet spot to come to  the formal plantings include clip domes  of yew trees  immaculate lawns and over 3 500 rose  bushes planted in a geometric pattern  around a central fountain it's all the  result of generations of royal  influence it's originally created by  george iv  extensively remodeled by queen victoria  and prince albert  but it's in the 20th century its current  look  was very much responsibility of the duke  of edinburgh so he effectively  redesigned it  in 1971 laying out the design  of the rose beds the queen's own  alterations were only temporary  as a child during world war ii she and  sister margaret cultivated crops in  their very own  victory garden the garden was completely  dug over  in order to plant vegetables and there  were two  separate uh plots set aside for the  young princess elizabeth  and her sister the princess margaret and  they grew sweet corn and tomatoes  beans as well i think all as part of the  war effort doing their doing their bit  it may be one reason why the area  remains special to her  but at the moment she's not around to  enjoy it after her own coronavirus  lockdown at windsor  the queen and prince philip have  decamped to their summer retreat  balmoral castle in scotland leaving  non-royals  to get the royal treatment it's pretty  cool right  very cool and the fact that it hasn't  been open in more than 40 years you  would think it would have been open this  whole time and now opening it during a  pandemic  doesn't seem i know necessarily sure  there'll be some precautions taken  i'm sure there will be cool place to see  though
 

